<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <PackageId>X3Code.Sql.Utils</PackageId>
        <Product>X3Code.Sql.Utils</Product>
        <Title>X3-Code SQL Utilities</Title>
        <Description>Lightweight ADO.NET access to SQL Server, without an ORM</Description>
        <PackageReleaseNotes>10.0.0: Split out of X3Code.Utilities. DatabaseConnector was the only type there that needed Microsoft.Data.SqlClient, and it dragged that whole graph - Azure.Identity, Microsoft.IdentityModel.* and the native SNI assets - into every project that just wanted the general purpose helpers. X3Code.Utilities has no package dependencies at all now.
BREAKING: the namespace is X3Code.Sql.Utils instead of X3Code.Utils.Database. Change the using; the API is otherwise the one described below.
Fixed: ExecuteSqlCommandOn never opened its connection and threw on every single call. The transactional overload additionally attached the transaction to a second, foreign connection and could not work either.
BREAKING: the transactional overload is now ExecuteSqlCommandOn(SqlTransaction transaction, string query, ...) and runs on the transaction's own connection - the ref keyword and the connection string are gone. Since the old signature threw on every call, no working code can depend on it.
Every method accepts SqlParameter values now, plus asynchronous overloads with a CancellationToken. Use the parameters instead of concatenating user input into the SQL string.
The SqlDataAdapter in SelectAsDataTable is disposed, and missing arguments raise ArgumentException instead of being swallowed.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
      <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
